          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Zariski topology
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topology
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+spectrum+of+a+commutative+ring"} prime spectrum of a commutative ring
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
          %p#Outline We consider the definition in increasing generality and sophistication:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                First we discuss the naive Zariski topology
                %a{:href => "#OnAffineSpace"} on affine spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi k
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^n
            %li
              %p
                then we turn to the modern definition of the Zariski topology
                %a{:href => "#OnAffineVarieties"} on affine varieties
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Spec
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R)
                which takes the concept of prime (and maximal) ideals as primary, and again we provide the classical arguments;
            %li
              %p
                finally we discuss the abstract
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretic
                perspective on these matters
                %a{:href => "#InTermsOfGaloisConnections"} in terms of Galois connections
                and obtain slick category theoretic proofs of all the previous statements.
          %p
            Starting with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+space"} affine space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^n
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subsets"} closed subsets
            those defined by the vanishing of any set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo ∀
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi i
