            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomials"} polynomials
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            of the topology are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complements"} complements
            of these
            = succeed "." do
              %em vanishing sets
          %p
            It is clear that the vanishing set such a set of polynomials depends only on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideal"} ideal
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polynomial+ring"} polynomial ring
            which is generated by them. Under this translation then forming the intersection of closed subsets corresponds to forming the sum of these ideals, and forming the union of closed subsets corresponds to forming the product of the corresponding ideals. This way the Zariski topology establishes a dictionary between topological concepts of the affine space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^n
          %p
            In particular one finds that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+closed+subsets"} irreducible closed subsets
            of the Zariski topology correspond to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideals"} prime ideals
            in the polynomial ring (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PrimeVanishingIdealOfIrreducibleZariskiClosed"}
            and prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PrimeIdealClosedsubspaceBijection"}
            below), and that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+points"} closed points
            correspond to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximal+ideals"} maximal ideals
            among these (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MaximalIdealsAreClosedPoints"}
          %p
            This motivates the modern refinement of the concept of the Zariski topology, where one considers any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
