              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and equips its set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+ideals"} prime ideals
            with a topology, by direct analogy with the previously naive affine space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            a polynomial ring and restricting attention to the maximal ideals (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AffinSpaceAsPrimeSpectrum"}
            below).
          %p
            These sets of prime ideals of a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            equipped with the Zariski topology are called the (topological spaces underlying) the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+spectrum+of+a+commutative+ring"} prime spectrum of a commutative ring
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spec
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R)
          %p
            The Zariski topology is in general not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff
            (example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AffineSpaceOverInfiniteFieldNotHausdorff"}
            below) which makes it sometimes be regarded as an “exotic” type of topology. But it is in fact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sober+topological+space"} sober
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ZariskiTopologyIsSober"}
            below) and hence as well-behaved in this respect as general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            are.
          %h2#OnAffineSpace On affine space
          %p
            We consider here, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
